Yes — I can see exactly what is happening from your screenshot.

The important part is this:

<mat-option
  [disabled]="isOptionDisabled(option)"
>
  <opti-checkbox
    ...
    (click)="$event.stopPropagation()"
    (checkboxChange)="toggleSelection(option)"
    [isDisabled]="isOptionDisabled(option)"
  >
  </opti-checkbox>
</mat-option>

You are disabling the mat-option, but the opti-checkbox is a separate interactive component inside it. Its checkboxChange can still fire.

So you need to handle this at the checkbox level as well as the option level.

1. First change your toggleSelection()

This is the most important fix.

Currently you have something like:

toggleSelection(option: any) {
  const current = this.control.value || [];

  if (this.isSelected(option)) {
    this.control.setValue(
      current.filter((item) => item.id !== option.id)
    );
  } else {
    this.control.setValue([...current, option]);
  }
}

Change it to:

toggleSelection(option: any): void {
  // Do nothing if the option is inactive
  if (this.isOptionDisabled(option)) {
    return;
  }

  const current = this.control.value || [];

  if (this.isSelected(option)) {
    this.control.setValue(
      current.filter((item) => item.id !== option.id)
    );
  } else {
    this.control.setValue([...current, option]);
  }
}

This guarantees that even if checkboxChange fires, the inactive option will never be added to your selected values.


---

2. Keep [disabled] on mat-option

Keep:

[disabled]="isOptionDisabled(option)"

So:

<mat-option
  *ngFor="let option of options"
  [value]="option"
  [disabled]="isOptionDisabled(option)"
  class="custom-option"
>

This handles the dropdown option itself.


---

3. Your checkbox

You currently have:

<opti-checkbox
  [checked]="isSelected(option)"
  (click)="$event.stopPropagation()"
  (checkboxChange)="toggleSelection(option)"
  [noFormGroup]="true"
  [title]="
    searchPlaceholder() === AddPanelType.BillingGroup
      ? option.name + ' (' + option.id + ')'
      : option.name
  "
  [isDisabled]="isOptionDisabled(option)"
>
</opti-checkbox>

If opti-checkbox actually supports isDisabled, keep it.

But based on your earlier statement that the component doesn't expose a disable property, I would not rely on this alone.

The toggleSelection() guard above is still required.


---

4. I would also change the click handler

Instead of:

(click)="$event.stopPropagation()"

use:

(click)="onCheckboxClick($event, option)"

Then add this to your TS:

onCheckboxClick(event: MouseEvent, option: any): void {
  if (this.isOptionDisabled(option)) {
    event.preventDefault();
    event.stopPropagation();
    return;
  }

  event.stopPropagation();
}

So your checkbox becomes:

<opti-checkbox
  [checked]="isSelected(option)"
  (click)="onCheckboxClick($event, option)"
  (checkboxChange)="toggleSelection(option)"
  [noFormGroup]="true"
  [title]="
    searchPlaceholder() === AddPanelType.BillingGroup
      ? option.name + ' (' + option.id + ')'
      : option.name
  "
  [isDisabled]="isOptionDisabled(option)"
>
</opti-checkbox>


---

5. Make sure isOptionDisabled() is correct

You haven't shown this method, but it should simply be:

isOptionDisabled(option: any): boolean {
  return option?.active === false;
}

Or, if you're using a more strongly typed object:

isOptionDisabled(option: { active?: boolean }): boolean {
  return option.active === false;
}

Don't use:

return !option.active;

unless you're certain active is always present.

Because:

undefined

would also become disabled with !option.active.


---

6. Your final relevant code

HTML

<mat-option
  *ngFor="let option of options"
  [value]="option"
  [disabled]="isOptionDisabled(option)"
  class="custom-option"
>
  <opti-checkbox
    [checked]="isSelected(option)"
    (click)="onCheckboxClick($event, option)"
    (checkboxChange)="toggleSelection(option)"
    [noFormGroup]="true"
    [title]="
      searchPlaceholder() === AddPanelType.BillingGroup
        ? option.name + ' (' + option.id + ')'
        : option.name
    "
    [isDisabled]="isOptionDisabled(option)"
  >
  </opti-checkbox>
</mat-option>

TS

isOptionDisabled(option: any): boolean {
  return option?.active === false;
}

onCheckboxClick(event: MouseEvent, option: any): void {
  if (this.isOptionDisabled(option)) {
    event.preventDefault();
    event.stopPropagation();
    return;
  }

  event.stopPropagation();
}

toggleSelection(option: any): void {
  // Safety check - inactive options can NEVER be selected
  if (this.isOptionDisabled(option)) {
    return;
  }

  const current = this.control.value || [];

  if (this.isSelected(option)) {
    this.control.setValue(
      current.filter((item) => item.id !== option.id)
    );
  } else {
    this.control.setValue([...current, option]);
  }
}

Why I'm recommending all three

You effectively have two controls:

mat-option
    |
    └── opti-checkbox

Disabling the parent mat-option doesn't necessarily disable the custom checkbox's internal interaction.

Therefore:

mat-option [disabled] → visually/semantically disables the option

onCheckboxClick() → prevents interaction with the checkbox

toggleSelection() guard → final safety net so inactive data can never enter your FormControl

That last one is especially important because even if the UI component behaves unexpectedly, your application state remains correct.